What:           /sys/class/amhdmitx/amhdmitx0/phy_inhibit
Date:           October 2026
Contact:        CoreELEC maintainers
Description:
                Persistent HDMI PHY output inhibition in the non-DRM HDMI 2.0
                driver. Read returns 0 or 1. Write accepts exactly 0 or 1, with
                an optional trailing newline. Initially 0.

                Writing 1 synchronously disables the PHY and prevents queued
                hotplug work, modesets, direct phy requests, receiver-sense
                probes, and resume from enabling it. HPD and EDID processing
                continue. Writes and PHY enable paths serialize on setclk_mutex.
                The PRBS diagnostic returns EBUSY while inhibited. Receiver-sense
                sampling pauses; rxsense_state reads 0 during inhibition.

                The existing SM1, TM2, and SC2 HPD interrupt handlers may still
                set PHY bandgap bias while inhibited. This is not a full PHY
                enable sequence; inhibition does not promise zero bias power.

                Writing 0 clears the inhibit. If hpd_state and ready are both 1
                and cur_VIC is known, the driver restores the programmed
                transport's SCDC and TX scrambling before enabling the PHY.
                Otherwise output stays off until a later ordinary modeset.
                Repeated writes of the same state are no-ops. Invalid input
                returns EINVAL.

                Switching to null or a non-HDMI output clears ready and
                cur_VIC under setclk_mutex. Consequently disp_mode reports
                VIC:0, and release cannot restore a disabled transport until
                an ordinary modeset establishes valid state again.

                This control does not select a resolution, validate sink video
                compatibility, or guarantee that the display has a picture.
                In particular, it does not recover an applied timing/format
                that becomes unsupported after a display capability change.
                Userspace must release the inhibit during exit and system sleep;
                a service supervisor should also release it after abnormal exit.
